# W4-NEXT-2 K: row 18, selecting a built house. Builds one pioneer house (public (0,0), id 501,
# construction_full_proof.script's own proven-free tile 1156,516), selects it, screenshots the
# panel, deselects with Escape, builds a second house at a neighbouring tile, selects it, then
# demolishes it while selected - selection.hpp's own "a building demolished meanwhile drops the
# selection" exercised through the real window, not just the unit test.
#
# F1 (2026-11-11 review, finding 1): every selection check below waits on the `selection.*` fact
# viewer.cpp's script_tick() builds from the live selection state, never on a fixed frame count -
# a fixed count races the sim thread's inspection answer under load (the review's own repro,
# `ctest -j 16`). A screenshot that follows such a wait also gets one more `wait frames 1`: the
# Windows capture (viewer.cpp's screenshot step) writes `buffer_`, the CPU path's own frame
# buffer, which is only as new as the window's last draw() call; draw() runs after script_tick()
# in the same loop pass (viewer.cpp's run loop), so a screenshot taken in the very tick the fact
# resolves would still read the frame drawn *before* that resolution. One more frame closes that
# gap (the next script_tick, after that pass's draw()) without weakening the wait itself.
resize 1024 768
wait frames 5
look 1140 516
key b
wait frames 5
click_px 833 330
wait frames 5
expect title build 501
click 1156 516
wait outcome
expect outcome Applied
checkpoint built_first

# Disarm the developer tool: while the menu is still open, a second B falls through to it
# (construction_handle's own key comment - the first B only opens the menu) and DevTool::handle's
# own Toggle flips `enabled_` false, since `arm()` had set it true (dev_tool.cpp). Cancel
# (the pickaxe/X click) does NOT clear this - selection needs the tool fully off
# (selection_active()'s own "!(dev_ enabled)"), not just the panel closed.
key b
wait frames 2
key esc
wait frames 5

# Select the first house: construction menu closed, the developer tool disarmed, a click on its
# tile falls to selection_handle.
click 1156 516
wait fact selection.kind == House within 5000
expect title selected House
wait frames 1
screenshot selected_house_live
checkpoint selected_first

# F2 (2026-11-11 review, finding 2): a click on the panel's own drawn UI - the need icon at
# (794,480), well inside it at (810,495) - is not a world click (selection.hpp's own doc comment);
# the selection must stay exactly as it is, with no new inspection request and no deselect.
click_px 810 495
wait frames 2
expect title selected House
checkpoint ui_click_keeps_selection

# Escape deselects (K-1): the title drops "selected", the panel stops drawing.
key esc
wait fact selection.selected == 0 within 5000
expect title-lacks selected
wait frames 1
screenshot deselected_live
checkpoint deselected

# Build a second house clear of the first's 4x4 footprint (1156..1159), select it instead of the
# first (only one selection at a time).
key b
wait frames 5
click_px 833 330
wait frames 5
expect title build 501
click 1164 516
wait outcome
expect outcome Applied
checkpoint built_second
key b
wait frames 2
key esc
wait frames 5
click 1164 516
wait fact selection.kind == House within 5000
expect title selected House
checkpoint selected_second

# Demolish the selected building while it is selected, with the developer tool still fully off
# (unchanged since the "key b"/"key esc" pair above - selection_active() still holds): a direct
# world command (`local demolish`, dev_script.cpp's own verb, which posts the command straight to
# the runner, bypassing dev_/the pickaxe entirely) so selection_refresh()'s own drop path (the
# selected id re-checked against the next inspection answer for its tile) is what resolves this
# title change, not DevTool's hover inspection. F3 (finding 6 of the 2026-11-11 review): the
# earlier version re-armed the tool first to reach the pickaxe, so selection_refresh() returned
# early (it drops `pending` and does nothing else while the tool is on) and this check passed for
# an unrelated reason - the tool's own hover inspection going empty, not the selection's drop path.
local demolish 1164 516
wait outcome
expect outcome Applied
wait fact selection.selected == 0 within 5000
expect title-lacks selected
checkpoint demolished_while_selected

# No trailing "key esc": unlike the pre-F3 version, the developer tool stays off from here to the
# end (F3's whole point), so selection_active() stays true with nothing selected - Escape's own
# table entry for that case ("without one: today's behaviour: quit") makes selection_handle return
# unconsumed, and the window's own default Escape binding would quit *before* this `quit` step
# ever ran, leaving DevScript::done() false and the window exiting 1 even though every check
# above passed (viewer.cpp's run loop; main.cpp's own "script failed or didn't finish" check).
quit
